--------------------------------------------------------------------
CRITERION 5 -- injection precision (EISDIR is NOT tolerated as ErrNotExist). PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Branch logic, internal/services/mcpsvc/curate/delete.go:118-124:
  p, err := deps.Snapshots.Ensure(f, now)
  if err != nil {
      if errors.Is(err, fs.ErrNotExist) { continue }          // tolerated
      return nil, deleteOutput{}, fmt.Errorf("refusing to write: could not back up %s: %w", f, err)   // abort
  }
and the mirror in upsert.go:180 (`if snapErr != nil && !errors.Is(snapErr,
fs.ErrNotExist) { ...skip the pin... }`). So the injection must produce an
error that is NOT fs.ErrNotExist, or the test would silently exercise the
tolerated branch instead of the one it names.
I did not take this on inspection. Command: a standalone Go probe
(`go run` in a scratch module) classifying each injection's real errno:
  directory-swap     err=read .../asdir: is a directory        | errors.Is(fs.ErrNotExist)=false | EISDIR=true
  dangling-symlink   err=open .../dangling: no such file...    | errors.Is(fs.ErrNotExist)=true  | EISDIR=false
  file-as-datadir    err=open .../f/child: not a directory     | errors.Is(fs.ErrNotExist)=false | ENOTDIR=true
  chmod-0000         bytes="secret" err=<nil>  (uid=0)
Three things follow, all mechanically demonstrated:
 - the curate directory-swap yields EISDIR, which is false for
   errors.Is(fs.ErrNotExist), so it lands in delete.go's abort branch /
   upsert.go's pin-skip branch -- the branches the two tests defend. Mutant
   (2) above independently confirms the code reaches that branch with the
   message "...is a directory".
 - a dangling symlink WOULD have been swallowed by the fs.ErrNotExist
   tolerance, so it is correctly NOT used in curate.
 - ENOENT via dangling symlink is nevertheless the right choice in
   internal/services/storage: rollbackDecryptionWithRecipient
   (migration.go:450-457) has no ErrNotExist tolerance at all -- ANY
   os.ReadFile error goes straight to `failed = append(failed, path)`.
   Mutant (1) proves the tests reach exactly that append.
 - the last probe line is the defect this task exists to fix: at uid 0 a
   chmod-0000 file is read straight through, err=<nil>. The old fixtures
   could not have failed as root.

